# Appendix E — The Synth & Audio DSP

Chapters 8, 9, and 14 taught the sound chip by ear. This appendix is the model
underneath: what a note *is* as data, how the synth turns your numbers into a
scored performance, and what the console hands back at the prompt versus what it
plays through a speaker in a live session.

## Everything musical is data first

The most important fact about Motoi's synth: a note, a chord, and a drum pattern
are all **data** — records you can print and read — before they are ever sound.
That is why the whole book could teach music headless: the synth builds a score
you can inspect, and a live console performs it.

## The tempo is the grid

Before any note, the console already keeps a beat. `audio/tempo` reports it:

```motoi
(display (audio/tempo))
(newline)
```

```
120
```

`120` beats per minute — the grid every note and drum hit lands on. This is the
same transport Chapter 9 built on; speed it up and the whole performance plays
faster, because every event is scheduled *relative to the beat*, not to wall time.

## A chord is a list of pitches, described in full

`synth/chord section pitches duration dynamic` builds one chord event. Pitches are
MIDI-style numbers — middle C is `60`, each semitone `+1`:

```motoi
(display (synth/chord 'lead '(60 64 67) 1 'mf))
(newline)
```

```
((:kind chord) (:section lead) (:pitches (60 64 67)) (:duration 1) (:dynamic mf))
```

Read the record: it is a `chord` on the `lead` section, pitches `60 64 67` (a
C-major triad), lasting `1` beat, at dynamic `mf` (mezzo-forte). Nothing here is
opaque — the event is a labelled list, so you can build, inspect, and transform
music with the same list verbs you use for everything else.

## A drum kit is a pattern string

`synth/kit name pattern voice` reads a row of ones and zeros into a hit pattern —
`1` is a strike, `0` a rest:

```motoi
(display (synth/kit 'acoustic \"1010\" 'kick))
(newline)
```

```
((:kind kit) (:name acoustic) (:steps 4) (:hits (0 2)) (:voice kick))
```

The string `\"1010\"` became a `4`-step pattern whose `kick` hits land on steps
`0` and `2`. The pattern string *is* the rhythm, readable at a glance: `\"1111\"`
drives, `\"1000\"` plods, `\"1011\"` stumbles. This is how a drum machine has
always worked, exposed as plain data.

## A score is a list of events the synth schedules

`synth/play score tempo` takes a list of events and schedules the whole
performance at your tempo, answering with a summary of what it laid down:

```motoi
(display (synth/play (list (synth/chord 'lead '(60) 1 'mf)) 120))
(newline)
```

```
(ok ((:kind score) (:events 1) (:tempo 120)))
```

You handed the synth a one-event score and it answered `ok` with a `score`
record: `1` event, tempo `120`. Hand it fifty events and it schedules fifty. The
return value is the *plan* — the honest description of what will play. On a live
console the transport pumps that plan and the notes actually ring.

## What runs at the prompt, and what needs a live console

The audio layer has two halves:

- **Building and scoring** — `synth/chord`, `synth/kit`, `synth/play`,
  `audio/tempo`. These are pure and honest at the bare prompt: they return the
  records above, which you can read and test. Every music example in this book
  lives here, which is why they all run.
- **Performing and analysing** — driving a real speaker, and the DSP verbs that
  listen back (`audio/play`, `audio/spectrum`, `audio/onset?`, `audio/lufs`).
  These need a running console with an audio device. At the bare prompt there is
  no speaker to drive and no signal to analyse, so you work with the score, not
  the sound.

This split is not a limitation of the model — it *is* the model. The synth is a
data structure you compose against; the live console is the thing that turns that
data into air.

> **Tip**
>
> Because music is data, you can build it with ordinary list verbs. `map` a
> transposition over a chord's pitches to shift a key; `append` two scores to
> join two phrases. A melody you can `map` over is a melody you can compose *with*
> — the synth never asks you to leave the language.

> **Warning**
>
> At the bare prompt, `synth/play` returns the score record but no sound leaves
> the machine — there is no speaker in a headless run. The `(ok …)` you see is the
> honest schedule, not silence hiding a bug. In a live console the Chapter 9 beat
> loop pumps that schedule and the notes ring. Read the record when testing; trust
> the console to sound it when it runs.

